📌 Java Introduction (Updated for Today)
✔️ Author / Origin

Author: James Gosling and team at Sun Microsystems (later acquired by Oracle Corporation)

Initial internal project name: Oak (named after a tree outside the office)

Present name: Java (chosen later, inspired by Java coffee)

✔️ Vendor

Originally: Sun Microsystems

Now: Oracle Corporation (Java is stewarded via OpenJDK and Oracle JDK distributions)

✔️ Type of Software

Java is open-source (via OpenJDK) and also has commercial builds from Oracle and other vendors

✔️ Platform Support

Java is platform-independent and runs on all major operating systems using the JVM (Write once, run anywhere)

✔️ Motto / Philosophy

WORA: Write Once, Run Anywhere — your Java code runs on any platform with a JVM without recompilation

✔️ Implementation Language

The JVM and core libraries are mostly implemented in C/C++, with new parts in Java and other languages in recent years.

✔️ Version Timeline (Key)

Beta versions: circa 1995

Initial release: JDK 1.0 (January 23, 1996)

Present major Java version: Java SE 26 (released March 2026)

Latest Long-Term Support (LTS): Java SE 25 (LTS) (released September 16, 2025)

✔️ Java Editions (Updated Names)

Modern Java platform is organized into editions, not “J2SE/J2EE/J2ME” anymore — those are older names.

Current Java Platform Editions

Java SE: Standard Edition (core language & libraries)

Jakarta EE: Enterprise Edition (server, enterprise APIs; formerly Java EE)

Java ME: Micro Edition (embedded & constrained devices)

Note: Oracle and Eclipse Foundation now use Java SE / Jakarta EE / Java ME, so the old terms like J2SE, J2EE, J2ME are outdated.

✔️ File Extensions

.java – Java source file

.class – Bytecode (compiled) file

.jar – Java archive (collection of compiled classes)

✔️ Compilation & Execution

Compilation: javac (Java compiler) compiles .java to .class bytecode

Execution: JVM (Java Virtual Machine) runs bytecode on any platform via the Java Runtime Environment (JRE)